    "https://jobs.adelaide.edu.au/jobs/communications-and-events-project-coordinator-adelaide-cbd-sa-australia": {
      "title": "Communications and Events Project Coordinator",
      "description": "Communications and Events Project Coordinator - Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia Manage your settings Search Jobs Visit adelaideuni.edu.au Kiosk mode Together, we will define Adelaide University Together, we will define Adelaide University Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Jobs near me Communications and Events Project Coordinator Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 HEO7 Fixed term - Full-time HEO7 Add to favourites View favourites Position Description Based in the Widening Participation and Impact team within the Internal and External Engagement portfolio, City East Campus Full time, fixed term contract until 27 July 2027 Salary Range: $103,719 – $116,002 per annum (plus 17% superannuation) At Adelaide University, we create the opportunities you need to achieve your ambitions – because when you thrive, we thrive. We are transforming education for contemporary learners and global citizens. Building on a proud legacy and shaped by bold ambition, it’s a place of excellence and equity, where our vibrant community of staff are united by our purpose to inspire Australia’s future change-makers and create a better tomorrow. Work that matters The Communications and Events Project Coordinator will be responsible for the coordination and delivery of the Children’s University (CU) Australasia and Africa (IO) communications and marketing activities, connecting with key stakeholders, members, learning destinations, industry and community partners. You will assist with developing and implementing engaging communications that promote CU opportunities, programs, media coverage and growth. Working collaboratively across South Australia and with national partners, you will build strong internal and external relationships and ensure consistent messaging, branding and communications. The role also oversees the development and enhancement of CU’s online platforms, ensuring they effectively support members and stakeholders. You will coordinate graduation ceremonies and other key events, manage the development of marketing and promotional materials, and oversee event and communications data to support effective planning and reporting. This is an opportunity to play a key role in growing the reach, engagement and visibility of Children’s University Australia. Curious to learn more? Explore the full role description below to discover more about this opportunity. The team Reporting to the Associate Director, Children’s University Australasia and Africa (IO), and working within the Widening Participation and Impact team, you will coordinate communications, digital engagement, events and stakeholder activities that help grow the reach and impact of Children’s University across Australia and internationally. Visit the AU website to learn more about Children's University. Our people Our people are guided by purpose, curiosity and a commitment to lifelong learning. We embrace authenticity, innovation and collaboration, and harness diverse thinking in our pursuit of excellence. This role is ideal for a proactive and creative communications professional who enjoys building relationships, coordinating engaging events and campaigns, and connecting people with opportunities that make a difference. This role is perfect for someone who enjoys variety and meeting new people. You will experience a range of tasks and interactions, keeping your workday interesting and engaging. Learn more about our people, what we stand for and what we offer at Careers at AU . Experience To join our community and thrive in this role, you will likely have the following skills and experience: Proven experience developing and implementing effective communication and marketing strategies across digital and print platforms. Experience developing, coordinating and managing events, with strong organisational and project management skills. Strong stakeholder engagement, negotiation and relationship-building skills, with the ability to collaborate and communicate effectively across all levels. Highly developed written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills, including the ability to present information clearly and persuasively. Demonstrated ability to analyse and interpret data, prepare reports and provide informed advice. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity We are committed to fostering a culture of inclusion where diversity is celebrated and everyone feels respected and valued. Adelaide University is an equal opportunity employer, committed to creating a safe, inclusive, and equitable workplace where everyone can thrive. We strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people with disability, and people of all ages, genders, cultural backgrounds, sexual orientations, and gender identities. We are committed to supporting flexible working arrangements and providing reasonable adjustments throughout the recruitment process. Launch your future with Adelaide University now Simply click on the Apply Now button and upload: your current CV a cover letter that tells us why you’re excited about the role The online application form will list the specific selection criteria that you need to address. Submit your application by 11:30pm Sunday 27 September 2026 For further information about this opportunity, please contact (quoting reference number 493491): Jessica Maslen Team Lead, Talent Acquisition, People & Culture +61 8 8302 1700 | careers.adelaideuniversity@adelaide.edu.au Applications welcomed from Australian or NZ citizens, Australian permanent residents and those who have the legal right to work in Australia for the term of appointment. Pursuant to the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (the Act) and the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Regulations 2019 (the Regulations), this position has been deemed prescribed. This role will require the successful candidate to hold a current Working with Children Check. #LI-DNI #S-DNI Recommended Jobs Triage Counsellor Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Student Success This opportunity forms part of Student Wellbeing and Safety team within the Student Success Function and is aligned to the Student Experience and Success Portfolio within the University. Team Lead, Quality and Compliance Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Services and Careers Lead a high-performing compliance team to strengthen payroll accuracy, manage audits, mitigate risk and drive continuous improvement across People & Culture. Technical Research Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 College Services - Engineering and Information Technology Support automotive safety research through the delivery of technical, laboratory and research activities Knowledge and Compliance Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Advisory and Wellbeing Support compliant pay practices through education, knowledge management and stakeholder engagement, helping drive accuracy, capability and compliance across Adelaide University. Adelaide University respectfully acknowledges the Kaurna, Boandik and Barngarla First Nations Peoples and their Elders past and present, who are the First Nations’ Traditional Owners of the lands that will be home to our campuses located in Adelaide, Magill, Mawson Lakes, Waite, Roseworthy, Mount Gambier and Whyalla. Disclaimer | Copyright | Privacy | Accessibility | Cookie Preferences Facebook Instagram LinkedIn Tiktok YouTube Weixin Australian University Provider Number PRV14404 | CRICOS Provider Number 04249J Candidate Details"
    },
    "https://jobs.adelaide.edu.au/jobs/software-engineer-adelaide-cbd-sa-australia": {
      "title": "Software Engineer",
      "description": "Software Engineer - Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia Manage your settings Search Jobs Visit adelaideuni.edu.au Kiosk mode Together, we will define Adelaide University Together, we will define Adelaide University Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Jobs near me Software Engineer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 HEO7 Information Technology Fixed term - Full-time HEO7 Add to favourites View favourites Position Description Based in the College of Engineering and Information Technology Full-time fixed term contract until 31 March 2028 Salary Range: HEO7 $103,719 - $116,002 per annum (plus 17% superannuation) At Adelaide University, we create the opportunities you need to achieve your ambitions because when you thrive, we thrive. We are transforming education for contemporary learners and global citizens. Building on a proud legacy and shaped by bold ambition, it’s a place of excellence and equity, where our vibrant community of staff are united by our purpose to inspire Australia’s future change-makers and create a better tomorrow. Join a high-impact research project at the forefront of next-generation computing technology. As a Software Engineer, you’ll design, develop and maintain the software infrastructure that enables technology assessment, benchmarking, testing and evaluation of advanced computing platforms. You’ll work hands-on with CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, SoCs/MPSoCs and accelerator-based architectures, building automated benchmarking capabilities, trusted toolchains, data collection systems and reproducible test environments. Work that matters This is an exciting opportunity to combine strong software engineering with interests in computer architecture, performance engineering and experimental systems research, while contributing to the evaluation of emerging technologies for demanding combat-system computing environments. You’ll play a key role in translating research concepts and candidate technologies into robust, instrumented and repeatable software/hardware test environments, with a focus on understanding performance and Space, Weight, Power and Cooling (SWaP-C) trade-offs. Curious to learn more? Explore the full role description to discover more about this opportunity. The team You’ll be part of a multidisciplinary team of researchers, engineers and Defence stakeholders working on the SWaP-C Optimisation Computer Technology Assessment research project. The team is focused on investigating, prototyping, integrating and evaluating current and emerging compute technologies, with an emphasis on practical, evidence-based assessment. Working collaboratively across research and engineering disciplines, you’ll help establish representative test and evaluation environments and an integrated, governed and version-controlled toolset to support future technology assessments. This is a collaborative environment where software engineering expertise directly contributes to research outcomes and helps shape the understanding and roadmap of advanced computing technologies. Visit the AU website to learn more about School of Computer Science and Information Technology. Our people Our people are guided by purpose, curiosity and a commitment to lifelong learning. We embrace authenticity, innovation and collaboration, and harness diverse thinking in our pursuit of excellence. This is an opportunity to join a vibrant team as we launch Australia’s new university for the future, Adelaide University. Learn more about our people, what we stand for and what we offer at Careers at AU . Experience To join our community and thrive in this role, you will likely have the following skills and experience: Relevant degree in Computer Science, Software Engineering, Computer Systems Engineering, Electrical/Electronic Engineering, or a closely related discipline, or equivalent professional experience. High-level software engineering capability in C/C++ and Python, including development, debugging, testing and maintenance of research or production-quality software. Strong experience with Linux-based systems, source control, build systems, scripting and automation, with desirable experience in CI/CD, containers and infrastructure automation. Demonstrated expertise in performance engineering, benchmarking, profiling and systems measurement, including designing repeatable experiments and analysing quantitative results. Knowledge of computer architecture and heterogeneous computing, with practical experience integrating hardware/software toolchains, drivers, SDKs, compilers, profilers or instrumentation across CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, SoCs/MPSoCs or accelerators; strong technical communication and multidisciplinary teamwork skills. Ability to deliver project outcomes to agreed milestones, handle sensitive project information and comply with security requirements Australian Citizenship and eligibility to obtain an NV1 clearance required Our commitment to inclusion and diversity We are committed to fostering a culture of inclusion where diversity is celebrated and everyone feels respected and valued. Adelaide University is an equal opportunity employer, committed to creating a safe, inclusive, and equitable workplace where everyone can thrive. We strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people with disability, and people of all ages, genders, cultural backgrounds, sexual orientations, and gender identities. We are committed to supporting flexible working arrangements and providing reasonable adjustments throughout the recruitment process. Launch your future with Adelaide University now Applying is simple. Simply click on the Apply Now button and upload: your current CV a cover letter that tells us why you’re excited about the role upload a document that includes your responses to all the experience criteria Submit your application by 11:30pm Sunday 27 September 2026 The University reserves the right to close this advertisement before the closing date if a suitable candidate is identified. For further information about this opportunity, please contact (quoting reference number 493427 ). Evelyn Ruth Talent Acquisition Advisor +61 8 8302 1700 | careers.adelaideuniversity@adelaide.edu.au Applications are welcomed from Australian citizens, residing in Australia only. Appointment is subject to a Personnel Security Clearance at NEGATIVE VETTING LEVEL 1 being obtained and maintained. #LI-DNI #S-DNI Ready to start your application? Apply here. First Name (required) 1b7c5953 Last Name (required) 5934b3e7 Email (required) 952bba18 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Ready to start your application? Apply here. First Name (required) 0d5d0be2 Last Name (required) fa3c043f Email (required) 944edd19 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Triage Counsellor Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Student Success This opportunity forms part of Student Wellbeing and Safety team within the Student Success Function and is aligned to the Student Experience and Success Portfolio within the University. Team Lead, Quality and Compliance Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Services and Careers Lead a high-performing compliance team to strengthen payroll accuracy, manage audits, mitigate risk and drive continuous improvement across People & Culture. Technical Research Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 College Services - Engineering and Information Technology Support automotive safety research through the delivery of technical, laboratory and research activities Knowledge and Compliance Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Advisory and Wellbeing Support compliant pay practices through education, knowledge management and stakeholder engagement, helping drive accuracy, capability and compliance across Adelaide University. Hear about jobs that are right for you! Sign up to job alerts First Name (required) cf7dcfac Last Name (required) 8a9ba4f3 Email (required) 30a5c056 Job Alert 9982b7d7 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories HEO7 Information Technology HEO7 Information Technology Locations Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia Portfolio College of Engineering and Information Technology College of Engineering and Information Technology Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Adelaide University respectfully acknowledges the Kaurna, Boandik and Barngarla First Nations Peoples and their Elders past and present, who are the First Nations’ Traditional Owners of the lands that will be home to our campuses located in Adelaide, Magill, Mawson Lakes, Waite, Roseworthy, Mount Gambier and Whyalla. Disclaimer | Copyright | Privacy | Accessibility | Cookie Preferences Facebook Instagram LinkedIn Tiktok YouTube Weixin Australian University Provider Number PRV14404 | CRICOS Provider Number 04249J Candidate Details Please choose a portfolio"
    },
    "https://jobs.adelaide.edu.au/jobs/research-fellow-plasma-seed-enhancement-b-waite-urrbrae-sa-australia": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow – Plasma Seed Enhancement (B) - Waite (Urrbrae), SA, Australia Manage your settings Search Jobs Visit adelaideuni.edu.au Kiosk mode Together, we will define Adelaide University Together, we will define Adelaide University Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Jobs near me Research Fellow – Plasma Seed Enhancement (B) Waite (Urrbrae), SA, Australia, 5064 Education & Training Level B Fixed term - Full-time Level B Add to favourites View favourites Based at the School of Agriculture, Food and Wine at the Waite Campus Full-time fixed term contract for 12 months Salary range: (Level B) $117,100 - $138,515 per annum (plus 17% superannuation) At Adelaide University, we create the opportunities you need to achieve your ambitions – because when you thrive, we thrive. We are transforming education for contemporary learners and global citizens. Building on a proud legacy and shaped by bold ambition, it’s a place of excellence and equity, where our vibrant community of staff are united by our purpose to inspire Australia’s future change-makers and create a better tomorrow. Work that matters We are seeking a Research Fellow – Plasma Seed Enhancement (B) to join the School of Agriculture, Food and Wine to research and scale up a new technology - Plasma Seed Enhancement (PSE). By improving seed germination and seedling establishment for Australian native plant seeds, PSE has the potential to increase the efficiency and success of native vegetation restoration, while supporting biodiversity and ecosystem resilience across Australia. Over the short 12-month duration of the project, Research Fellow will have accumulated ample evidence to demonstrate a significant advance in the technology readiness of PSE, going from bench-top to small pilot scale, and positioning PSE for commercial readiness. The team Reporting to Associate Professor Bryan Coad, you will work within a collaborative, multidisciplinary research environment alongside researchers, students and industry partners to deliver high-impact research outcomes. This role is based within the School of Agriculture, Food and Wine at the Waite Campus, home to world-class programs in agricultural science, viticulture and oenology, and food science. As Australia’s leading agricultural research precinct, the Waite Campus brings together teaching, research and industry collaboration to drive innovation in agriculture. Our aim is to equip graduates and industry partners with the knowledge, skills and insights needed to support sustainable food and horticultural systems. Visit the AU website to learn more about The School of Agriculture, Food and Wine. Our people Our people are guided by purpose, curiosity and a commitment to lifelong learning. We embrace authenticity, innovation and collaboration, and harness diverse thinking in our pursuit of excellence. AProf Coad’s plasma laboratory houses several systems used for driving new technological advances in food processing, sustainable biomaterials, and seed enhancement processes for agriculture and horticulture. In this project, germination outcomes for plasma treated seeds will be evaluated by specific biological, physical and chemical methods (e.g. seed germination assays, measurements of water uptake, and surface-sensitive analytical techniques). While many of these techniques are located at Waite campus, some will be accessed at other Adelaide University campuses. A major objective of the role is to demonstrate scale-up of the technology and so the candidate will evaluate treatment results from small and mid-sized plasma systems. This role is ideal for a skilled researcher who also has an entrepreneurial mindset where the ideal combination of skills would include excellence in technical research and scale-up that will scope the pathway from the laboratory to commercial operation. Learn more about our people, what we stand for and what we offer at Careers at AU . Experience To join our community and thrive in this role, you will likely have the following skills and experience: Essential A PhD in Chemistry, Physics, Engineering, Materials Science or a closely related discipline, or equivalent demonstrated research experience relevant to the project. Demonstrated experience in experimental research involving the operation, development or optimisation of technical scientific equipment, including the ability to troubleshoot equipment and develop robust experimental protocols. Demonstrated experience in designing, conducting and analysing quantitative experiments, including the ability to collect, interpret and critically evaluate experimental data and draw scientifically sound conclusions. Demonstrated ability to independently plan and deliver research activities within defined timelines, including managing competing priorities, maintaining accurate research records, and meeting project milestones and deliverables. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in multidisciplinary and industry-engaged research environments, including communicating technical information to researchers, industry partners and other stakeholders, and contributing to collaborative problem-solving. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity and inclusion, and ability to contribute to the diversity of the school community Desirable Demonstrated capacity to translate research into practical applications, including contributing to technology scale-up, commercialisation and/or entrepreneurship, and to the progression of a technology from laboratory or bench scale towards pilot-scale application. Experience with plasma technology, plasma processing, surface modification or related technologies . Experience in seed science, plant science, horticulture, agriculture or ecological restoration , including experience with seed germination, plant establishment or assessment of plant growth and vigour. Evidence of research outputs and/or impact in a closely related field , such as peer-reviewed publications, industry reports, patents, commercialisation outcomes or other demonstrated contributions to research translation. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity We are committed to fostering a culture of inclusion where diversity is celebrated and everyone feels respected and valued. Adelaide University is an equal opportunity employer, committed to creating a safe, inclusive, and equitable workplace where everyone can thrive. We strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people with disability, and people of all ages, genders, cultural backgrounds, sexual orientations, and gender identities. We are committed to supporting flexible working arrangements and providing reasonable adjustments throughout the recruitment process. Launch your future with Adelaide University now Applying is simple. Simply click on the Apply Now button and upload: your current CV a cover letter that tells us why you’re excited about the role a statement (word document) that addresses the essential selection criteria Submit your application by 11:30pm, Wednesday 23 September 2026 Please note that the role description is not attached to this advertisement as it is currently being finalised. The University reserves the right to close this advertisement before the closing date if a suitable candidate is identified. For further information about this opportunity, please contact (quoting reference number 493453) : Jessica Maslen Team Lead – Talent Acquisition People and Culture +61 8 8302 1700| careers.adelaideuniversity@adelaide.edu.au Applications welcomed from Australian or NZ citizens, Australian permanent residents and those who have the legal right to work in Australia for the term of appointment. #S-DNI #LI-DNI Recommended Jobs Triage Counsellor Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Student Success This opportunity forms part of Student Wellbeing and Safety team within the Student Success Function and is aligned to the Student Experience and Success Portfolio within the University. Team Lead, Quality and Compliance Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Services and Careers Lead a high-performing compliance team to strengthen payroll accuracy, manage audits, mitigate risk and drive continuous improvement across People & Culture. Technical Research Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 College Services - Engineering and Information Technology Support automotive safety research through the delivery of technical, laboratory and research activities Knowledge and Compliance Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Advisory and Wellbeing Support compliant pay practices through education, knowledge management and stakeholder engagement, helping drive accuracy, capability and compliance across Adelaide University. Adelaide University respectfully acknowledges the Kaurna, Boandik and Barngarla First Nations Peoples and their Elders past and present, who are the First Nations’ Traditional Owners of the lands that will be home to our campuses located in Adelaide, Magill, Mawson Lakes, Waite, Roseworthy, Mount Gambier and Whyalla. Disclaimer | Copyright | Privacy | Accessibility | Cookie Preferences Facebook Instagram LinkedIn Tiktok YouTube Weixin Australian University Provider Number PRV14404 | CRICOS Provider Number 04249J Candidate Details"
